---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Num from '../components/Num.astro';
import Verdicts from '../components/Verdicts.astro';
import { runs, base } from '../lib/data';
const all = runs();
const primary = all.find((r) => r.mode === 'pipeline' && r.dataset === 'newer_college_bag9' && r.variant === 'baseline' && r.profile === 'laptop-full');
---
<Layout title="Overview" description="A reproducible study of whether scan-to-scan LiDAR odometry meets a 10 Hz sensor's 100 ms deadline.">
  <section class="wrap section">
    <p class="caption">A · Research overview</p>
    <h1 style="max-width:18ch">Can LiDAR odometry keep up with a 10 Hz sensor?</h1>
    <p style="font-size:18px;margin-top:16px;max-width:70ch">A reproducible study measuring whether scan-to-scan LiDAR odometry can meet a 10 Hz sensor's 100 ms deadline, what it costs in accuracy, and what it takes to close the gap, built before committing to robot hardware.</p>
    <p class="muted">It is a measurement study, not a navigation system. Every result is labelled with the hardware it ran on.</p>
    <div class="grid cols-3" style="margin-top:24px">
      <div class="card stat"><div class="caption">Sensor period</div><div class="value">100 ms</div><p class="muted">1 / 10 Hz. A frame that takes longer delays every later frame.</p></div>
      <div class="card stat"><div class="caption">Primary dataset</div><div class="value">OS1-64</div><p class="muted">Newer College 2020, Ouster OS1-64 Gen1, 64 beams, 10 Hz, with ground truth.</p></div>
      {primary && <div class="card stat"><div class="caption">Baseline median latency</div><div class="value"><Num run={primary.run_id} path="latency_ms.total_ms.p50" digits={0} /> ms</div><p class="muted">Development laptop, no limits. {primary.hardware_label}.</p></div>}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <h2>Current answer</h2>
    {primary ? (
      <>
        <p class="muted">Verdicts below are generated by documented rules from the run's measurements (<span class="mono">{primary.run_id}</span>). They describe this machine and this dataset only.</p>
        <Verdicts run={primary.run_id} />
      </>
    ) : <p class="notice">No run has been published yet. Run <code>autonav run</code>, then <code>autonav publish &lt;run-id&gt;</code> and export the site data.</p>}
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <h2>How the question is answered</h2>
    <div class="grid cols-2">
      <div class="card"><h3>Pipeline</h3>
        <svg role="img" aria-label="Pipeline: dataset adapter, preprocessing, ICP registration, validity gate, trajectory, evaluation, artifacts, site" viewBox="0 0 640 120" width="100%" style="max-width:640px">
          {['Dataset', 'Preprocess', 'ICP', 'Gate', 'Pose', 'Evaluate'].map((t, i) => (
            <g transform={`translate(${i * 106},30)`}><rect width="94" height="44" rx="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#e5e5e5" /><text x="47" y="27" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#0a0a0a">{t}</text></g>
          ))}
          <text x="0" y="104" font-size="11" fill="#737373">Python writes run artifacts (JSON/CSV/binary). This site only renders them.</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="muted">Each stage is timed separately, in a fresh process per repeat, after warm-up frames are excluded.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Method</h3>
        <ul style="padding-left:18px;margin:0">
          <li>Scan-to-scan point-to-point ICP with a validity gate; accuracy against ground truth where it exists.</li>
          <li>Latency percentiles and the deadline <em>violation rate</em>, not just a percentile.</li>
          <li>Three algorithm variants side by side: baseline, optimized, point-to-plane.</li>
          <li>Hardware profiles: full laptop, 4 cores, 1 core. ARM cloud and Raspberry Pi 5 are listed separately.</li>
        </ul></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <h2>Scope and limits</h2>
    <ul style="padding-left:18px;max-width:78ch">
      <li>Measurements come from ordinary Python on a Windows laptop. They are not hard real-time guarantees and say nothing about other hardware, except the one-way inference stated in <a href={base + 'hardware/'}>section G</a>.</li>
      <li>Odometry only: no loop closure, no IMU fusion, no mapping. Error accumulates with distance.</li>
      <li>Two datasets with different sensors. Oxford Spires (Hesai QT64) is never presented as an OS1-64 result.</li>
      <li>Unverified: ground-truth frame offset (see <a href={base + 'algorithm/'}>section C</a>), companion-computer performance, flight conditions.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Layout>